## Why This Is Asked
Tests classic DP with an additional dimension (at most k intervals) and reconstruction—a common pattern in scheduling optimization problems.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint (at most k intervals)
- Predecessor p(i) computed via binary search on sorted end times
- DP table with an extra dimension tracking the number of selected intervals
- Backtracking reconstruction to output the chosen jobs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueKJobs(jobs, k) {
  const sorted = jobs.map((j, idx) => ({ ...j, idx })).sort((a, b) => a.end - b.end);
  const n = sorted.length;
  const ends = sorted.map(j => j.end);

  const p = (i) => {
    let lo = 0, hi = i - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
      const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
      if (ends[mid] <= sorted[i].start) lo = mid + 1;
      else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return hi;
  };

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const prev = p(i - 1) + 1;
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][t], dp[prev][t - 1] + sorted[i - 1].value);
    }
  }

  const selected = [];
  let t = k;
  for (let i = n; i > 0 && t > 0; i--) {
    if (dp[i][t] !== dp[i - 1][t]) {
      selected.push(sorted[i - 1]);
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], jobs: selected.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Realistic sprint planning with bounded weekly windows; tests ability to form and optimize DP with small constant branching and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for fast range totals
- DP with fixed window length (2..7)
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time O(n) with a small constant, Space O(n)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minWeeklyCost(A, W) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(INF);
  const par = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  dp[0] = 0;
  for (let i=2; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let k=2; k<=7; k++) {
      if (i-k < 0) break;
      const sum = pref[i] - pref[i-k];
      const cost = dp[i-k] + (sum - W)*(sum - W);
      if (cost < dp[i]) { dp[i] = cost; par[i] = i-k; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would follow using par[]
  return {cost: dp[n], prev: par[n]};
}
```

